-534.events:
=== By place ===
==== Europe ====
* [[Lucius Tarquinius Superbus]] becomes the seventh king of Rome, after murdering his predecessor [[Servius Tullius]].{{Citation needed|date=February 2026}}
* Competitions for tragedy are instituted at the City Dionysia festival in [[Athens]].{{Citation needed|date=February 2026}}
* The [[Etruscan civilization|Etruscan]] founded the city of Felsina, that later will become the modern city of [[Bologna]].{{Citation needed|date=February 2026}}

-532.events:
=== By place ===
==== Europe ====
* [[Pythagoras]] arrives in the Greek [[Colonies in antiquity|colony]] of Croton (modern-day [[Crotone]]) in [[Magna Graecia]] and founds the philosophical school of [[Pythagoreanism]] (approximate date).{{Citation needed|date=February 2026}}

39.events:
=== 30 AD ===

==== South Asia ====

* The [[Kushan Empire]] is founded (approximate date).{{Citation needed|date=February 2012}}

==== Roman Empire ====

* [[Agrippina the Elder]] (the wife of [[Germanicus]]) and two of her sons, [[Nero Julius Caesar]] and [[Drusus Caesar]], are arrested and [[Exile|exiled]] on orders of [[Sejanus|Lucius Aelius Sejanus]] (the prefect of the [[Praetorian Guard]]), and later [[Starvation|starved]] to death in suspicious circumstances. In Sejanus's purge of Agrippina the Elder and her family, her son [[Caligula]], and her three daughters, [[Agrippina the Younger]], [[Julia Drusilla]] and [[Julia Livilla]] are the only survivors.<ref>Suetonius, ''The Lives of Twelve Caesars'', Life of Tiberius, pp. 53–54.</ref>
* [[Phaedrus (fabulist)|Phaedrus]] translates [[Aesop's fables]], and composes some of his own.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.enotes.com/topics/phaedrus|title=Phaedrus Biography - eNotes.com|website=eNotes|language=en|access-date=2018-01-11}}</ref>
* [[Velleius Paterculus]] writes the general history of the countries known in [[Classical antiquity|Antiquity]].{{Citation needed|date=February 2012}}

==== Palestine ====
* Jesus is crucified in Jerusalem (possible date)<ref>[https://www.catholic.com/audio/caf/the-accurate-date-of-the-crucifixion Jimmy Akin, The Accurate Date of the Crucifixion]</ref>

=== 31 AD ===

==== Roman Empire ====

* [[Sejanus|Lucius Aelius Sejanus]] is named co-[[Consul]] to Emperor [[Tiberius]].  However, Tiberius becomes aware of Sejanus' treachery and has him arrested and executed.{{Citation needed|date=June 2026}}
* [[Naevius Sutorius Macro]] becomes the leader of the [[Praetorian Guard]] after Sejanus is executed.{{Citation needed|date=June 2026}}

=== 32 AD ===

==== Egypt ====

* [[Philo]] writes his symbolic interpretation of the Old Testament ''(Allegory)''.{{Citation needed|date=June 2026}}

=== 33 AD ===

==== Roman Empire ====

* Emperor [[Tiberius]] founds a credit bank in [[Rome]].<ref>{{cite book|last=Harris|first=W. V.|title=Rome's Imperial Economy: Twelve Essays|year=2011|publisher=Oxford University Press|isbn=978-0-19-959516-7|page=238|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=LLy8ckG_AC0C&dq=Tiberius+credit+bank+33&pg=PA238}}</ref>
* [[Financial crisis of 33|A financial crisis hits Rome]], due to poorly chosen fiscal policies. Land values plummet, and credit is increased. These actions lead to a lack of money, a crisis of confidence, and much [[land speculation]]. The primary victims are senators, knights and the wealthy. Many aristocratic families are ruined.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Thornton|first1=M. K.|last2=Thornton|first2=R. L.|date=1990|title=The Financial Crisis of A.D. 33: A Keynesian Depression?|url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/2122822|journal=The Journal of Economic History|volume=50|issue=3|pages=655–662|doi=10.1017/S0022050700037207|jstor=2122822|s2cid=154785575|issn=0022-0507|url-access=subscription}}</ref>

==== China ====

* Although the usurpation of [[Wang Mang]] and the [[Chimei|Chimei Rebellion]] are behind him, [[Emperor Guangwu of Han|Emperor Guangwu]] now faces a new threat to the [[Han dynasty]]: the [[Naval history of China#Early literature|Rebellion of Gongsun Shu]] in the [[Sichuan]] province. Gongsun's naval forces are unsuccessful against Han General Cen Peng, so Gongsun decides to fortify his position by [[Blockade|blockading]] the entire [[Yangtze River]] with a large floating [[pontoon bridge]], complete with floating fortified posts. After Cen Peng is unable to break through, he constructs several "[[Naval history of China#Tower ships|castle ships]]" with high [[Defensive wall|ramparts]] and [[ramming]] vessels known as "colliding swoopers", which break through Gongsun's lines and allow Cen to quell his rebellion. Gongsun Shu is totally defeated [[AD 36|three years later]].{{Citation needed|date=June 2026}}

==== Palestine ====

* According to [[Colin Humphreys#Date of Last Supper|Colin Humphrey]]'s account, [[Jesus of Nazareth]]'s [[Last Supper]] and crucifixion take place.<ref name="humphreyssupper">{{Cite book|first=Colin J.|last=Humphreys|title=The Mystery of the Last Supper|publisher=Cambridge University Press|date=2011|isbn=978-0521732000|pages=77 and 189}}</ref><ref name="bbcsupper">{{Cite web|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-13114124|title=Last Supper 'was on a Wednesday'|date=April 18, 2011|location=United Kingdom|publisher=BBC}}</ref>

=== 34 AD ===

==== Roman Empire ====

* The [[Roman aqueduct]] [[Pont du Gard]] is constructed, running for 50&nbsp;km, ending at [[Nîmes]].{{Citation needed|date=June 2026}}
* [[Naevius Sutorius Macro]] gains favour in the [[Roman Empire]] by [[Prostitution|prostituting]] his wife [[Ennia Thrasylla]] to [[Caligula]].{{Citation needed|date=June 2026}}
* Rome intervenes in [[Armenia]] (AD 34–[[AD 37|37]]).{{Citation needed|date=June 2026}}

==== Europe ====

* The original inhabitants of [[Dacia]] revolt against the [[Sarmatians|Sarmatian]] tribe of [[Iazyges]], who had [[Slavery|enslaved]] them.{{Citation needed|date=June 2026}}
* According to one dating scheme, Paul and Barnabas announce in [[Antioch]] that they will begin to evangelize the Gentiles.{{Citation needed|date=June 2026}}

==== Palestine ====

* [[Saint Stephen|Stephen]], one of the original seven deacons of the Christian Church, is martyred for his faith (possible date).{{Citation needed|date=June 2026}}

==== Syria ====

* [[Paul the Apostle|Saul]] of Tarsus, on the road to [[Damascus]], is converted to [[Christianity]], and becomes [[Paul the Apostle]] (possible date).{{Citation needed|date=June 2026}}

=== 35 AD ===

==== Roman Empire ====

* [[Tiberius|Emperor Tiberius]] names [[Caligula]] (aka Gaius) and Gamellus, a grandson, as co-heirs.<ref name="BBC">{{cite web |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/history/historic_figures/tiberius.shtml|access-date=February 20, 2024|title=Tiberius (42 BC-37AD|publisher=BBC}}</ref>
* [[Pliny the Elder]] is brought to [[Ancient Rome|Rome]] before this year.{{Citation needed|date=June 2026}}

==== Persia ====

* [[Tiridates III of Parthia|Tiridates III]] becomes king of [[Parthia]] (until [[AD 36]]).{{Citation needed|date=June 2026}}

=== 36 AD ===

==== China ====

* [[December 25]] &#x2013; [[Wu Han (Han dynasty)|Wu Han]] commands the forces of Emperor [[Emperor Guangwu of Han|Guang Wu]] of the [[Han dynasty#Eastern Han|Eastern Han]] to conquer the separatist [[Chengjia]] Empire, reuniting [[China]].<ref name="Crespigny2006">{{cite book|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=49OvCQAAQBAJ&pg=PA270|title=A Biographical Dictionary of Later Han to the Three Kingdoms (23–220 AD)|last=de Crespigny|first=Rafe|author-link=Rafe de Crespigny|date=2006|publisher=BRILL|isbn=978-90-474-1184-0|page=270}}</ref>

==== Roman Empire ====

* [[Pontius Pilate]] is recalled to [[Ancient Rome|Rome]], after putting down a [[Samaritan]] uprising.{{Citation needed|date=June 2026}}
* [[Lucius Vitellius the Elder|Lucius Vitellius]] defeats [[Artabanus III (of Parthia)|Artabanus III]] of [[Parthia]] in support of another claimant to the throne, [[Tiridates III of Parthia|Tiridates III]].{{Citation needed|date=June 2026}}
* [[Herod Antipas]] suffers major losses in a war with [[Aretas IV Philopatris|Aretas IV]] of [[Nabatea]], provoked partly by Antipas' divorce of Aretas' daughter. According to [[Josephus]], Herod's defeat was popularly believed to be divine punishment for his execution of [[John the Baptist]]. Emperor [[Tiberius]] orders his governor of [[Syria (Roman province)|Syria]], Vitellius, to capture or kill Aretas, but he is reluctant to support Herod and abandons his campaign upon Tiberius' death in [[AD 37]].<ref>[[Josephus]], <i>[[Antiquities_of_the_Jews]]</i> [https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/cgi-bin/ptext?lookup=J.+AJ+18.113 18.113&#x2013;126]; {{cite journal |last=Bruce|first=F. F.|author-link=F. F. Bruce|year=1963–1965|title=Herod Antipas, Tetrarch of Galilee and Peraea|journal=Annual of Leeds University Oriental Society|volume=5|pages=6&ndash;23, pp. 17&ndash;18|url=http://www.biblicalstudies.org.uk/pdf/herod_bruce.pdf|archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20221009/http://www.biblicalstudies.org.uk/pdf/herod_bruce.pdf|archive-date=2022-10-09|url-status=live|access-date=2007-10-21}}</ref>

==== Palestine ====

* [[Marcellus (prefect of Judea)|Marcellus]] becomes governor of [[Iudaea Province|Judaea]] and [[Samaria]].{{Citation needed|date=June 2026}}

=== 37 AD ===

==== Roman Empire ====

* [[March 18]] &#x2013; The [[Roman Senate]] annuls [[Tiberius]]'s will, and proclaims [[Caligula]] as [[Roman Emperor]],<ref name="cambridge">{{cite book|last=Bowman|first=Alan K.|last2=Champlin|first2=Edward|last3=Lintott|first3=Andrew|title=The Cambridge ancient history: The Augustan Empire, 43 B.C.–A.D. 69|url=https://archive.org/details/cambridgeancient32boar|url-access=limited|year=1996|publisher=Cambridge University Press|isbn=978-0-521-26430-3|page=[https://archive.org/details/cambridgeancient32boar/page/n238 221]}}</ref> nullifying the joint claim of [[Tiberius Gemellus]]. Caligula's attempt to have himself [[Imperial cult (ancient Rome)|deified]] creates friction between himself and the Senate.{{Citation needed|date=June 2026}}
* [[October]] &#x2013; Caligula becomes seriously ill, or perhaps is poisoned. He recovers from his illness, but Caligula turns toward the diabolical: he starts to kill off those who are close to him, whom he sees as a serious threat.{{Citation needed|date=June 2026}}
* [[Abilene (biblical)|Abilene]] is granted to King [[Agrippa I]].{{Citation needed|date=June 2026}}

==== Anatolia ====

* [[Saint Peter|Peter the Apostle]] founds the [[Church of Antioch]] (approximate date).{{Citation needed|date=June 2026}}
* [[April 9]] &#x2013; An [[earthquake]] destroys [[Antioch]].<ref>{{cite book|last=Downey|first=Glanville|title=A history of Antioch in Syria: from Seleucus to the Arab conquest|year=1961|publisher=Princeton University Press|page=190}}</ref>
* The [[Pharisee]] [[Saul of Tarsus]] is converted to [[Christianity]], after a vision (possible date). After the year [[AD 39|39]], he is recognised as [[Paul of Tarsus|Saint Paul]].{{Citation needed|date=June 2026}}

=== 38 AD ===

==== Roman Empire ====

* [[Claudius]] and [[Valeria Messalina|Messalina]] are most likely married at this point in time.
* [[Apion]] heads a deputation to Emperor [[Caligula]], to complain about the [[Jew|Jews]] in [[Alexandria]].
* [[Phaedrus (fabulist)|Phaedrus]] writes his popular collection of [[fables]].

==== Egypt ====

* An [[Alexandrian riots (38 CE)|anti-Jewish riot breaks out in Alexandria]], during a visit by King [[Herod Agrippa|Herod Agrippa I]]; the mob wants to place statues of Caligula in every [[synagogue]].

==== Anatolia ====

* [[Andrew the Apostle]] becomes the first [[Ecumenical Patriarch of Constantinople|patriarch of Constantinople]], and resigns that position shortly thereafter (traditional date).
* [[Stachys the Apostle]] becomes the second [[Ecumenical Patriarch of Constantinople|patriarch of Constantinople]] (traditional date).

==== China ====

* An [[epidemic]] breaks out in K'aui-chi, causing many deaths. Imperial official Ch'ung-li I provides medicines that save many lives.{{Citation needed|date=January 2022}}

=== 39 AD ===

==== Roman Empire ====

* [[Tigellinus]], minister and favorite of the later [[Roman emperor]] [[Nero]], is banished for [[adultery]] with [[Caligula]]'s sisters.{{Citation needed|date=June 2026}}
* [[Caligula|Gaius Caesar Augustus Germanicus (Caligula)]] and [[Gnaeus Domitius Corbulo]] become [[Roman consul|Roman consuls]].{{Citation needed|date=June 2026}}
* Caligula orders a [[Pontoon bridge|floating bridge]] to be built using ships as [[Pontoon (boat)|pontoons]], stretching for two miles from [[Baiae]] to the neighboring port of [[Pozzuoli|Puteoli]].{{Citation needed|date=June 2026}}
* [[Philo]] leads a [[Jewish]] delegation to Rome to protest the anti-Jewish conditions in [[Alexandria]].{{Citation needed|date=June 2026}}

==== Germania ====

* [[Legio XV Primigenia|Legio XV ''Primigenia'']] and [[Legio XXII Primigenia|XXII ''Primigenia'']] are levied by Caligula for the [[Germany|German]] frontier.{{Citation needed|date=June 2026}}
* Caligula's campaign into Germany is stopped by a conspiracy led by [[Cassius Chaerea]]. Even though he never even reaches Germany, Caligula proclaims himself victorious and orders a triumph.{{Citation needed|date=June 2026}}

==== Palestine ====

* [[Agrippa I]], king of [[Judea|Judaea]], successfully accuses [[Herod Antipas]], tetrarch of [[Galilee]] and [[Perea (Holy Land)|Perea]], of conspiracy against Caligula. Antipas is exiled and Agrippa receives his territory.<ref>[[Josephus]], <i>[[Antiquities_of_the_Jews]]</i> [https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/cgi-bin/ptext?lookup=J.+AJ+18.247 18.247&#x2013;252]; {{cite journal |last=Bruce|first=F. F.|author-link=F. F. Bruce|year=1963–1965|title=Herod Antipas, Tetrarch of Galilee and Peraea|journal=Annual of Leeds University Oriental Society|volume=5|pages=6&ndash;23, p. 21|url=http://www.biblicalstudies.org.uk/pdf/herod_bruce.pdf|archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20221009/http://www.biblicalstudies.org.uk/pdf/herod_bruce.pdf|archive-date=2022-10-09|url-status=live|format=[[Portable Document Format|PDF]]|access-date=2007-10-21}}</ref> 
* [[Caligula]] orders that a statue of himself be placed in the [[Temple in Jerusalem]]. The governor of [[Syria]], Publius Petronius, who is responsible for having an erecting  the statue, faces mass demonstrations by Jews of the region and manages to delay construction of the statue until the death of Caligula in [[AD 41]].{{Citation needed|date=June 2026}}

==== Vietnam ====

* The [[Trung Sisters]] resist the [[China|Chinese]] influences in [[History of Vietnam|Vietnam]].{{Citation needed|date=June 2026}}

50.events:
=== 50 AD ===

==== Roman Empire ====

* [[History of Cologne|Cologne]] is raised to the status of a city.{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}
* [[Utrecht]] is founded, and a Roman fortification (''[[castellum]]'') is constructed at the [[Limes (Roman Empire)|Rhine border]] in the present-day [[Netherlands]].{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}
* [[Claudius]] adopts [[Nero]].{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}
* Roman emperor [[Claudius]] appoints [[Agrippa II]] governor of [[Chalcis]].{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}

==== Palestine ====

* In [[Judaea (Roman province)|Judaea]], a [[Roman Empire|Roman]] soldier seizes and burns a [[Torah]] scroll. [[Procurator (Roman)|Procurator]] [[Cumanus]] has the culprit beheaded, calming down local [[Jews]] and delaying for almost two decades the outbreak of their revolt.<ref>[[Flavius Josephus]], "Ant." xx. 5, § 4; "B. J." ii. 12, § 2.</ref>
* The [[Twelve Apostles|Apostles]] hold the [[Council of Jerusalem]] (approximate date).{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}

==== Greece ====

* Paul travels to [[Philippi]], [[Thessaloniki]], [[Beroea]], and [[Roman Athens|Athens]] ([[second missionary journey]]).{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}
* The [[Epistle to the Romans]] is written (approximate date).{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}
* [[Hero of Alexandria]] invents a [[steam turbine]] (possible date).{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}
* [[Pamphilus of Alexandria]] writes a poetic lexicon.{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}
* [[Pedanius Dioscorides]] describes the medical applications of plants in ''De Materia Medica''.{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}

==== Britain ====

* In [[Roman Britain|Britain]], governor [[Publius Ostorius Scapula]] begins his campaign against the recalcitrant [[Silures]] of south [[Wales]], who are led by the former [[Catuvellauni|Catuvellaunian]] prince [[Caratacus]]. [[London]] (''[[Londinium]]''), [[Exeter]] (''[[Isca Dumnoniorum]]''), [[Tripontium]] (near modern [[Rugby, Warwickshire|Rugby]]) and the fort of [[Manduessedum]] (near modern [[Atherstone]]) are founded (approximate date).{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}
* Romans build a [[London Bridge|wooden bridge]] across the [[River Thames|Thames]] in the London area.{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}

==== Nubia ====

* [[Christianity]] is introduced throughout [[Nubia]] by a high official of Queen Judith.{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}

==== South Asia ====

* The [[Yuezhi]] tribes are united under the [[Kushan Empire|Kushan]] leader [[Kujula Kadphises]], thus creating the [[Kushan Empire]] in Afghanistan and northern India (approximate date).{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}

==== Americas ====

* The [[San Bartolo (Maya site)|San Bartolo]] pyramid is completed around this time.{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}

=== 51 AD ===

==== Roman Empire ====

* Emperor [[Claudius]] and future emperor [[Vespasian|Titus Flavius Vespasianus]] become [[List of Early Imperial Roman Consuls|Roman Consuls]].{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}
* [[Sextus Afranius Burrus|Burrus]], [[praetorian prefect]] (51–62 AD), charges [[Seneca the Younger|Seneca]] with the education of [[Nero]].{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}

==== Britain ====

* In [[Roman Britain|Britain]], governor [[Publius Ostorius Scapula]] [[Caratacus' last battle|defeats]] [[Caratacus]] and the [[Silures]] in the territory of the [[Ordovices]] in central [[Wales]].{{efn|[[Oxford University Press|Oxford's]] ''The Kings and Queens of Britain''—among others—date the battle as happening in [[AD 50]].<ref name="Cannon&Hargreaves2009">{{cite encyclopedia |last1=Cannon |first1=John |author-link1=John Cannon (historian) |last2=Hargreaves |first2=Anne  |author-link2= |date=2009 |title=Caratacus |url=https://www.oxfordreference.com/view/10.1093/acref/9780199559220.001.0001/acref-9780199559220-e-387 |encyclopedia=The Kings and Queens of Britain |location= |publisher=[[Oxford University Press]] |access-date=2 August 2026 |isbn=9780191727252 |via=[[Oxford Reference]] |edition=2nd}}</ref>}} Caratacus seeks sanctuary with [[Cartimandua]], queen of the [[Brigantes]] in northern England, but she is a Roman ally and hands him over to [[Publius Ostorius Scapula|Ostorius]].<ref>{{cite encyclopedia |last1=Kerr|first1=Anne|last2=Wright|first2=Edmund|date=2015|title=Caratacus|url=https://www.oxfordreference.com/view/10.1093/acref/9780199685691.001.0001/acref-9780199685691-e-665|encyclopedia=A Dictionary of World History|location=|publisher=[[Oxford University Press]]|access-date=2 August 2026|isbn=9780191765728|via=[[Oxford Reference]]}}</ref> Despite the defeat, the Silures continue to fight.
* The captured Caratacus is exhibited in chains in Claudius' [[Roman triumph|triumph]] in [[Ancient Rome|Rome]], but his dignified demeanour persuades the emperor to spare his life and allow his family to live free in the [[Capital city|capital]] for a short period of time.{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}

==== Parthia ====

* [[Vonones II of Parthia|Vonones II]] dies a few months after he had ascended to the throne. His son Vologases I becomes the new Parthian king.<ref name=":0">{{cite encyclopedia |last=Morby|first=John|date=2014|title=Parthian Empire|url=https://www.oxfordreference.com/view/10.1093/acref/9780191780073.001.0001/acref-9780191780073-e-178|encyclopedia=Dynasties of the World|location=|publisher=[[Oxford University Press]]|access-date=2 August 2026|edition=2nd|isbn=9780191780073|via=[[Oxford Reference]]}}</ref>

=== 52 AD ===

==== Roman Empire ====

* Emperor [[Claudius]] attempts to control the [[Fucine Lake]] by digging a 5.6&nbsp;km [[tunnel]] through ''Monte Salviano'', requiring 30,000 workers and eleven years.{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}
* Claudius completes the ''Aqua Claudia'' and ''Anio Novus'', two aqueduct projects started by [[Caligula]].<ref>{{cite book | first=John E. | last=Stambaugh | title=The Ancient Roman City | place=Baltimore | publisher=Johns Hopkins University Press | year=1988 | isbn=0-8018-3574-7|page=130}}</ref>
* In [[Ancient Rome|Rome]], a [[Roman law|law]] prohibits the execution of old and crippled [[History of slavery|slaves]].{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}
* [[Barea Soranus]] is ''consul suffectus'' in Rome.{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}
* [[Pliny the Elder]] writes his account of the German wars.{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}

==== Palestine ====

* [[Ananias son of Nedebaios|Ananias]], a high priest in [[Jerusalem]], is sent to [[Rome]] after being accused of violence.{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}

==== Britain ====

* In [[Roman Britain|Britain]], governor [[Publius Ostorius Scapula]] dies while campaigning against the [[Silures]] of south [[Wales]]. Following his death, the Roman [[Legio II Augusta|Second Legion]] are heavily defeated by the Silures. His replacement is [[Aulus Didius Gallus]], who quells the rebellion and consolidates the gains the Romans have so far made, but does not seek new ones.{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}

==== India ====

* [[Thomas the Apostle|Saint Thomas]], one of the twelve disciples of [[Jesus]], is believed to have landed in [[Kodungallur]], [[India]] to preach the [[Good news (Christianity)|Gospel]]; the [[Syro-Malabar Catholic Church]], the [[Malankara Mar Thoma Syrian Church]], the [[The Malankara Orthodox Syrian Church|Indian Orthodox Church]], and the [[Assyrian Church of the East]] claim descent from him.{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}

==== China ====

* The ''Yuejue Shu'', the first known [[gazetteer]] of [[China]], is written during the [[Han dynasty|Han Dynasty]].{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}

=== 53 AD ===

==== Roman Empire ====

* Emperor [[Claudius]] removes [[Herod Agrippa II]] from the tetrarchy of [[Chalcis]] in [[Greece]].{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}
* [[Decimus Junius Silanus Torquatus]] and [[Quintus Haterius Antoninus]] become [[Roman consul|Roman consuls]].{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}
* Claudius secures a senatorial decree that gives jurisdiction in financial cases to imperial [[Procurator (Roman)|procurators]].  This marks a significant strengthening of imperial powers at the expense of the [[Roman Senate|Senate]].{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}
* [[June 9]] &#x2013; [[Nero]] is adopted by Claudius as his son and marries his 14-year-old daughter [[Claudia Octavia]].{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}
* Claudius accepts Nero as his successor, to the detriment of [[Britannicus]], his son by his first wife, [[Valeria Messalina]].{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}
* [[Seneca the Younger|Seneca]] writes the tragedy ''[[Agamemnon (Seneca)|Agamemnon]]'', which he intends to be read as the last chapter of a trilogy including two of his other tragedies, ''[[Medea (Seneca)|Medea]]'' and ''[[Oedipus (Seneca)|Edipus]]''.{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}

==== Anatolia ====

* [[Paul the Apostle]] writes his [[epistle to the Galatians]] from [[Ephesus]] (approximate date).{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}

==== Syria ====

* [[Evodius]] succeeds [[Saint Peter]] as [[List of Patriarchs of Antioch|Patriarch of Antioch]].{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}

==== Britain ====

* [[Timeline of Cardiff history|Cardiff]] is founded by [[Aulus Didius Gallus]].{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}

==== Caucasus ====

* [[Tiridates I of Armenia|Tiridates I]], brother of [[Vologases I]], comes to power in [[Armenia]] as an adversary of the Romans.<ref>Daryaee, Touraj. ''The Oxford Handbook of Iranian History'' Oxford University Press, 2012, p. 175</ref>

==== Korea ====

* [[Taejodae of Goguryeo|Taejodae]] becomes ruler of the kingdom of [[Goguryeo]].<ref name="met53">{{cite web |title=List of Rulers of Korea|url=https://www.metmuseum.org/toah/hd/koru/hd_koru.htm|website=www.metmuseum.org|access-date=18 April 2019}}</ref>

=== 54 AD ===

==== Roman Empire ====

* [[October 13]] &#x2013; Emperor [[Claudius]] dies (possibly after being poisoned by [[Agrippina the Younger|Agrippina]], his wife and niece), and is succeeded by [[Nero]].<ref name="Claudius">{{cite web |title=BBC – History – Claudius|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/history/historic_figures/claudius.shtml|website=www.bbc.co.uk|access-date=8 February 2020}}</ref>
* Nero attempts to prohibit the [[Gladiator|gladiatorial games]].{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}

==== Anatolia ====

* [[Gnaeus Domitius Corbulo]] arrives in the East and takes up an assignment as governor of [[Asia (Roman province)|Asia]], with a secret [[Letter (message)|brief]] from Nero and his chief ministers, [[Seneca the Younger|Seneca]] and [[Sextus Afranius Burrus|Burrus]], to return [[Armenia]] to the [[Roman Empire]].{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}
* Winter &#x2013; Domitius Corbulo marches his legions ([[Legio VI Ferrata]] and Legio X) into the mountains of [[Cappadocia]] and makes camp. He gives the men a harsh training, twenty-five-mile marches and weapons drills.{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}
* [[Patriarch]] [[Onesimus]] succeeds [[Stachys the Apostle]] as [[Ecumenical Patriarch of Constantinople|Patriarch of Constantinople]].{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}
* [[Apollos]], a later assistant of Paul, is converted to Christianity in  [[Ephesus]].{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}

==== Syrian ====

* Corbulo inspects a base of [[Legio X Fretensis]] in [[Syria]], at [[Cyrrhus]]; the [[Roman legion|Roman legionaries]] are demoralized by a "long peace". Many soldiers  sell their helmets and [[Scutum (shield)|shields]].{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}
* Corbulo recruits Syrian [[Auxilia|auxiliary]] units in the region and stations them in border [[Castra|forts]], with orders from Nero not to provoke the [[Parthia|Parthians]].{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}

==== Palestine ====

* Palestine is returned piecemeal to [[Herod Agrippa]]'s son [[Herod Agrippa II|Marcus Julius Agrippa]] between 48 and 54.{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}
* Violence erupts in [[Caesarea Maritima|Caesarea]] regarding a local ordinance restricting the [[civil rights]] of Jews, creating clashes between Jews and pagans. The Roman garrison, made up of Syrians, takes the side of the pagans. The Jews, armed with clubs and swords, meet in the marketplace. The governor of Judea, [[Antonius Felix]], orders his troops to charge. The violence continues and Felix asks Nero to arbitrate. Nero sides with the pagans, and relegates the Jews to second-class citizens.{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}

