* Emperor [[Emperor Guangwu of Han|Guang Wu]] grants [[Nakoku]] (located around modern-day [[Fukuoka City]]) a golden seal, being the oldest evidence of [[writing]] in [[Japan]]. In return King Na sends an envoy to [[China]].{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}
* [[March 29]] &#x2013; Guang Wu dies after a 32-year reign and is succeeded by his son [[Emperor Ming of Han|Han Mingdi]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Mingdi {{!}} emperor of Han dynasty|url=https://www.britannica.com/biography/Mingdi|website=Encyclopedia Britannica|access-date=18 April 2019|language=en}}</ref>

==== Korea ====

* Accession of King [[Talhae of Silla|Talhae]] as Korean ruler of [[Silla]].<ref name="met57">{{cite web |title=List of Rulers of Korea|url=https://www.metmuseum.org/toah/hd/koru/hd_koru.htm|website=www.metmuseum.org|access-date=18 April 2019}}</ref>

=== 58 AD ===

==== Roman Empire ====

* Emperor [[Nero]] and [[Marcus Valerius Messala Corvinus (consul 58)|Marcus Valerius Messalla Corvinus]] become [[List of Early Imperial Roman Consuls|Roman consuls]].{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}
* The friendship between Nero and [[Otho|Marcus Salvius Otho]] ends when they both fall in love with [[Poppea Sabina]], and Otho is sent to [[Lusitania]] as governor.{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}
* [[Agrippina the Younger]] is expelled from the imperial palace by her son Nero, who installs her in [[Roman villa|Villa Antonia]] in [[Misenum]], and leaving more of the effective and real power of [[Empire]] in the hands of [[Nero]].{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}
* The [[Ficus Ruminalis]] begins to die (see [[Rumina]]).
* [[Agrippina the Younger]], conspired with the senators in late 58 to overthrow [[Nero]].

==== Palestine ====

* The apostle [[Paul of Tarsus|Paul]] returns to [[Jerusalem]] with the money he has collected to give the Christian community there. However, he is accused of defiling the temple, and is arrested and imprisoned in [[Caesarea Maritima|Caesarea]]. He then invokes his [[Roman citizenship]] and is sent to [[Ancient Rome|Rome]] to be judged.{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}

==== Caucasus ====

* [[Roman–Parthian War of 58–63|Roman-Parthian War]]: [[Gnaeus Domitius Corbulo]], commander in the East, launches his Armenian offensive against [[Parthia]]. He leads a Roman army (four [[Roman legion|legions]]) through the mountainous country of [[Armenia]], against the fortress at Volandum, to the southwest of [[Artaxata]]. After a siege of eight hours Corbulo takes the city; the [[Legionary|legionnaires]] massacre the defenders and plunder Volandum to their hearts' content.{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}
* Corbulo marches to Artaxata crossing the [[Aras River]]; along the valley he is shadowed by tens of thousands of mounted Parthian archers led by king [[Tiridates I of Armenia|Tiridates I]]. The city opens its gates to Corbulo, just as it had to [[Germanicus]] four decades before. When he takes the 250-year-old Armenian capital, Corbulo gives the residents a few hours to collect their valuables and burns the city to the ground.{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}

==== Britain ====

* [[Gnaeus Julius Agricola]], 18 years old, is serving as a military [[tribune]] in [[Roman Britain|Britain]] under [[Gaius Suetonius Paulinus]] and is attached to [[Legio II Augusta|Legio II ''Augusta'']].{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}

==== Europe ====

* In [[Thuringia]], conflict between two [[Germanic tribes]] erupts over access to water.{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}
* [[Roman Empire|Romans]] learn the use of [[soap]] from the [[Gauls]] (approximate date).{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}

==== China ====

* Emperor [[Emperor Ming of Han|Ming of Han]] introduces [[Buddhism]] to [[China]] inviting monks from the western [[Indus Valley]].{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}
* In China, sacrifices to [[Confucius]] are ordered in all government schools.<ref>Roberts,  J: "History of the World.". Penguin, 1994.</ref>
* Start of ''Yongping'' era of the Chinese [[Han dynasty]].{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}

=== 59 AD ===
[[File:Nero_Agrippina_aureus_54.png|thumb|Coin of [[Nero]] and [[Agrippina the Younger]]]]

==== Roman Empire ====

* [[March 23]] &#x2013; Emperor [[Nero]] orders the murder of his mother [[Agrippina the Younger]]. He tries to kill her through a planned [[shipwreck]], but when she survives, he has her executed and frames it as a [[suicide]].{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}
* [[Publius Clodius Thrasea Paetus]] retires from the [[Roman Senate]]. He openly shows his disgust at the behaviour of Nero regarding the murder of Agrippina.{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}
* Rioting breaks out between the people of [[Pompeii]] and the people of [[Nocera Inferiore|Nuceria]] during a [[gladiator]] fight in Pompeii. Thousands are killed.{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}
* In the ''[[Satyricon]]'', [[Petronius]] pokes fun at Roman immorality.{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}

==== Palestine ====

* [[Paul the Apostle]] pleads his case and testifies to his [[Christianity]] before King [[Agrippa II]] of the [[Herodians]], who reportedly responds "''You almost persuade me to be a Christian''."<ref>[[New_Testament]], ''[[Acts of the Apostles|Acts]]'' 26.</ref>

==== Caucasus ====

* [[Gnaeus Domitius Corbulo]], [[Imperial Rome|Roman]] commander in the east, captures [[Tigranocerta]] in [[Mesopotamia]]. He installs [[Tigranes VI of Armenia|Tigranes VI]], a [[Cappadocia|Cappadocian]] prince, as ruler of [[Armenia]]. For the next four years, a [[Cohort (military unit)|cohort]] from [[Legio VI Ferrata]] and [[Legio X Fretensis]] is stationed in the capital as bodyguard to the king, supported by fifteen hundred [[Auxilia|auxiliaries]].{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}

==== North Africa ====

* An [[List of solar eclipses in the 1st century|eclipse]] on 30 April over North Africa is recorded by Pliny the Elder in his Natural History.{{Citation needed|date=August 2026}}

